e(a[st:ed],'> [!tip] Choose features, then test the workload\n> Compare required resize operations, repair tooling, snapshots, redundancy and supported configurations. On CoW storage, database page size, record size, compression and update patterns interact. Benchmark the actual database and durability settings before selecting a record size; no fixed value is optimal for every deployment.','Replace unqualified workload rankings and tuning prescriptions.',z+ext+x)
e('In 1991, Mendel Rosenblum and John Ousterhout observed two trends.','In their early-1990s LFS work, Mendel Rosenblum and John Ousterhout considered two trends.','Avoid publication-version ambiguity.',l)
e('a disk that does 100 random I/Os a second.','a disk whose random I/O is limited by seek and rotation.','No identified device supporting rate.',l)
e("The cleaner is LFS's Achilles' heel. Cleaning a segment that is a fraction *u* live costs reading it and rewriting *u* of it to gain (1 − *u*) of free space, exactly the write amplification maths of SSD garbage collection. LFS's **cost-benefit** policy prefers cleaning cold segments even if they're fuller, because their free space stays free longer.","Cleaning a segment that is a fraction *u* live requires reading it and rewriting its live fraction to reclaim (1 − *u*) of capacity. In a steady-state model excluding metadata, total writes per unit of new data are 1/(1 − *u*); counting reads as well is a different I/O-cost measure. LFS's **cost-benefit** policy balances segment age and utilization to favor reclaimed space likely to remain useful.",'Separate write amplification from total I/O and qualify cleaner policy.',l)
e(' Many Android phones use it.','','Remove unverified prevalence claim.',['https://docs.kernel.org/filesystems/f2fs.html'])
