e('(TCP port 2049).','(commonly TCP port 2049; NFSv3 can use other transports and auxiliary RPC services).','NFSv3 transport/ports broader.',n)
e('the **server keeps no per-client state**.','the core file-access protocol avoids per-client **open-file state**. Caches, unstable writes and separate locking services can still involve state.','Stateless slogan not literally zero state.',n)
e('Most operations are **idempotent**: repeating `READ` or `WRITE` at an offset gives the same result. So when a reply is lost, the client just retries.','Operations such as reading or writing a fixed byte range support simple retry models, but concurrent changes can alter read results or be overwritten by a repeated write. Lost replies require attention to operation semantics and duplicate handling.','Idempotence does not guarantee unchanged reads amid concurrency.',n)
e('The payoff is simple crash recovery: a rebooted server has no state to rebuild, and clients just retry until it answers. That is what the default **hard** mount does.','Avoiding core open-file state simplifies crash recovery. A default **hard** mount retries timed-out requests indefinitely, subject to other error and interruption behavior; this does not mean the server has no persistent state to recover.','Server persistence and hard mount scope.',n)
e('if a `REMOVE` succeeds but the reply is lost, the retry fails with "no such file".','if a `REMOVE` succeeds but the reply is lost, re-executing it can return "no such file" when nobody recreates the name.','Duplicate handling and concurrent recreation exceptions.',n)
e("So if A closes a file and B opens it afterwards, B sees A's writes.","With close-to-open behavior enabled, successful writeback/close and no intervening writer, B's later open revalidates A's changes. Applications must check write, fsync and close errors.",'Consistency needs successful operation and no later overwrite.',n)
e('A file deleted on the server while a client uses it gives `ESTALE` (stale file handle).','A file handle that no longer identifies a valid server object can produce `ESTALE`; deletion and client-side open-file handling have implementation-dependent details.','Deletion not unconditional ESTALE.',n)
e('the server can hand a client exclusive rights to a file,','the server can grant a read or write delegation,','Read delegations need not exclusive.',nf)
e('sessions (exactly-once semantics for retries)','sessions (duplicate-request handling within session, slot and replay-cache rules)','Avoid unlimited exactly-once business effect claim.',nf)
e("**HDFS**, Hadoop's open-source re-implementation, follows the same design:","**HDFS**, Hadoop's open-source system inspired by GFS, follows related design ideas:",'Not interchangeable protocol/implementation.',h+g)
e('| Unit | 64 MB chunk | 128 MB block |','| Unit | 64 MiB chunk in the paper | commonly 128 MiB block; configurable |','Explicit binary units and configuration.',h+g)
e('**One master holds all metadata in RAM**: the namespace, each file\'s list of blocks, and where every replica lives. Mutations are recorded in an operation log (HDFS calls it the edit log) and periodic checkpoints.','**A master serves each namespace**: it holds the namespace, file-to-block mappings and reported replica locations in memory. Namespace mutations are logged and checkpointed. HDFS reconstructs block locations from DataNode reports rather than persisting every location in its edit log.','Persistence and location-report distinctions.',h)
e('a 1 TB file is only about 8,000 blocks of 128 MB','a 1 TiB file is exactly 8,192 blocks of 128 MiB','Exact binary arithmetic.',h)
e("so each node's network link is used once.","so one payload copy travels along each pipeline hop; intermediate nodes receive and forward data, and acknowledgements travel back.",'Each node link used for receive and transmit.',h)
e('With replication 3, HDFS puts one replica','With replication 3 and enough eligible nodes across racks, the default policy puts one replica','Placement requires topology and eligible storage.',h)
e('HDFS simplified to single-writer, write-once files (with append added later).','HDFS uses a single-writer model, with supported append and truncate operations rather than arbitrary in-place byte updates.','Append and truncate supported.',h)
e('Every file, directory and block is an object in the NameNode\'s heap (a widely quoted rule of thumb is about 150 bytes each). A hundred million small files can need tens of gigabytes of heap.','Namespace entries and blocks consume NameNode memory, with size depending on Hadoop version, JVM and metadata. A large small-file population can exhaust heap despite modest payload size.','Unsupported universal bytes-per-object removed.',h)
e('Modern HDFS runs an active and a standby NameNode sharing the edit log through a quorum of JournalNodes, with ZooKeeper for failover.','HDFS can use active/standby NameNodes with shared edits; a JournalNode quorum is one documented approach, and ZooKeeper supports automatic failover when configured.','HA components depend on configured design.',h)
e('survives any 3 losses and costs 50% overhead, at the price of more CPU and network on reads after a failure.','can reconstruct any three missing cells in a stripe and has 50% ideal payload overhead for full stripes. Short files, metadata and placement affect actual capacity; reading missing data requires reconstruction work. In the cited Hadoop 3.3.5 documentation, striped EC files do not support append or truncate, and `hflush`/`hsync` do not provide ordinary replicated-file sync behavior.','Failure unit and versioned EC operation restrictions.',ec)
e('Google replaced GFS with **Colossus**, which distributes the metadata itself. Today many analytics workloads use **object storage** (S3, GCS) instead of HDFS: it separates storage from compute and scales metadata, but it isn\'t a file system. There is no in-place modification, no append, and "rename a directory" is a copy of every object under it.','Object stores can separate storage from compute, but their APIs are not interchangeable with POSIX filesystems. Capabilities depend on product and bucket type: S3 Express One Zone directory buckets support object append, and Cloud Storage hierarchical-namespace buckets support folder rename. Check the actual API instead of assuming every object store requires copying a whole prefix to rename it.\n\n> [!note] Deployment evidence gap\n> No measured NameNode heap profile or identified storage benchmark accompanies this lesson. Fixed bytes-per-object and guaranteed filesystem/SLOG latency claims are omitted. Quiz numbers explicitly marked as assumptions are arithmetic models, not observed deployments.','Remove unsupported deployment claims and false blanket object-store limitations; expose evidence gap.',obj)
e("ext4 and XFS are journaled, mature and fast; XFS's allocation groups suit parallel workloads but it can't shrink. Neither checksums file data.","ext4 and XFS journal metadata. XFS uses allocation groups for parallel allocation and has limited, version-dependent shrink support; their ordinary metadata checksums do not protect mutable file payload.",'Keep recap consistent.',ext+x)
e('ZFS and Btrfs are copy-on-write with end-to-end checksums, snapshots and built-in RAID; ZFS adds a pool model, the ARC and a separate intent log for fast `fsync`.','ZFS and Btrfs offer CoW, configurable integrity protection, snapshots and integrated redundancy. ZFS adds a pool model, ARC and an intent log that may use separate log devices.','Avoid configuration/performance guarantee in recap.',z+bt)
e('NFSv3 keeps the server stateless and operations idempotent for easy recovery, with close-to-open consistency;','NFSv3 avoids core open-file state and uses retries with duplicate handling, with close-to-open consistency;','State and idempotence qualification.',n)
